# Check every text file out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows checks out CRLF by default. Several tests assert
# on the shape of the source and read it with `include_str!`, which embeds the
# file exactly as checked out — so patterns containing "\n" matched nothing and
# four tests failed on every Windows run. They never failed on a developer
# machine, so it went unnoticed until Dependabot opened a pull request and CI
# ran on windows-latest.
#
# The tests also normalise line endings themselves, so neither this file nor a
# local `core.autocrlf` setting can break them again.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or they will not run; the installer is downloaded
# and executed, so a stray CR is a broken install rather than a cosmetic issue.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf

# PowerShell too. It is LF today and the Windows CI job that installs and runs
# it passes, so there is nothing to fix — and this project has already shipped
# two Windows *encoding* bugs (1.2.0 and 1.3.0, both fatal to terminal access,
# both reported as one unhelpful message). Introducing a platform-dependent
# line ending in a script that is embedded with `include_str!` and then written
# to disk is not a change to make without a reason.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# Never touch binaries.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff2 binary
*.dmg binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.odt binary
